# Codex execution rules for the agent-toolkit pipeline wrappers.
#
# Why this file exists: under a Codex lead, every OpenCode dispatch, status
# check and wait used to need its own runtime approval, because the OpenCode
# CLI writes under its own home directory and reaches the local server —
# both outside the workspace sandbox. A long run asked dozens of times, and
# users ended up hand-adding these exact allow rules to their personal
# ~/.codex/rules/default.rules. This file is that allowlist, project-scoped
# and reviewable.
#
# Scope is deliberately the toolkit's own dispatch/check wrappers and
# nothing else. Each is a script in this repository that resolves server,
# port and credential in one place. Git writes, protected-config writes,
# and anything else still prompt: spec approval and merge consent are
# workflow gates, and runtime approval for those stays a separate control.
#
# Codex loads project .codex/ layers (config, hooks, rules) only for a
# trusted project, and these rules let the named commands run outside the
# sandbox. Review this file the way you review hooks, and treat any change
# to it as a permission change (the toolkit-update gate covers it). A rule
# that reads correctly is not proof it is enforced: after scaffolding,
# confirm live that `scripts/oc.sh --status` runs without a prompt and that
# an unlisted command (e.g. `git push`) still prompts. Offline check:
#   codex execpolicy check --rules .codex/rules/pipeline.rules -- scripts/oc.sh --status
#
# Invoke the wrappers by their repo-relative path (`scripts/oc.sh …`), not
# through `bash scripts/…` or an absolute path — the rule matches the
# command prefix.

prefix_rule(
    pattern = [[
        "scripts/oc.sh",
        "scripts/bg-dispatch.sh",
        "scripts/claude-review.sh",
        "scripts/verify-models.sh",
    ]],
    decision = "allow",
    justification = "agent-toolkit dispatch wrappers reach the local OpenCode server or the Claude CLI",
)
